well here in the valley clark county and  the state are splitting one million  dollars in new funding it will expand  the unlv immigration clinic over the  next two years now they provide free  legal services to both children and  adults facing deportation hector mejia  has the story  with this new funding the unlv  immigration clinic now plans to open its  first permanent off-campus location  they're in the process of hiring staff  and choosing that location  handprints of actual clients line the  hallways of the unlv immigration clinic  these are children who were granted the  right to stay in the u.s the whole legal  process can take several years this is  critical for keeping families together  to make sure that people don't have an  empty chair at the dinner table for kids  who've seen or experienced awful things  to make sure they don't have to go back  to the people who attacked them most of  the clients here involve children  unaccompanied minors who fled horrendous  gang violence and social turmoil that's  according to director michael kagan the  clinic has about 150 active cases kagan  says while the 1 million dollars in new  funding from clark county and the state  is not going to meet every need it's a  huge step forward no matter what you  think about whether people should or  should not be deported i hope people can  agree that the process at least has to  be fair and this process cannot be fair  if people don't have legal advice we are  told someone is five times more likely  to avoid deportation if they have a  lawyer if you know someone who needs  legal advice you can call the clinic  directly their phone number is  702-895-2080 reporting from unlv hector  mejia live local now
 

